    "text_delta": "dbooks/union agreements).\n* Look for rules regarding **panel composition**, such as mandatory gender balance, diversity representation, or the presence of an independent outside assessor.\n\n#### Step 3: Seek Guidance from Your Union or Staff Representative\n* If you are a member of a union (such as the NUJ, MEAA, CWA, or relevant national equivalent) or a staff association, consult your representative confidentially.\n* They often know whether procedural rules were breached and can advise on whether informal inquiries or a formal grievance is appropriate.\n\n#### Step 4: Ask for Objective Data in Your Feedback\nWhen you do receive feedback or attend a debrief:\n* Ask how the competency framework was weighted between editorial leadership experience and specific digital skills.\n* Request your scores across each competency category relative to the benchmark required for the role.\n\n---\n\n### Summary\n\n**Is it a sign of anything?** Yes. At minimum, an all-male panel at a public broadcaster is a red flag for poor recruitment governance and an environment where unconscious bias can operate unchecked.\n\n**Should you make something of it?** Not as an unsubstantiated accusation right away. Instead, focus first on securing your formal feedback, checking whether panel composition rules were violated, and consulting a union or HR advisor to determine whether formal procedural action is justified.\n",
